  <h1>Is Poparide available in the USA?</h1>
  <div class="meta">Updated August 2026</div>

  <p class="lede"><strong>Short answer: no.</strong> Poparide — Canada's largest city-to-city carpool platform — operates across British Columbia, Alberta, Ontario, Quebec and beyond, but it has never launched in the United States. If you've used it for a Vancouver–Whistler or Toronto–Montreal trip and you're looking for the same thing south of the border, here's the honest landscape.</p>

  <h2>What Poparide does — and where it stops</h2>
  <p>Poparide matches passengers with drivers already making an intercity trip, with seat prices set to share the cost of the drive rather than turn a profit. It's the dominant app in that space in Canada, especially on routes like Vancouver–Kelowna, Calgary–Edmonton and Toronto–Ottawa. Its coverage ends at the border: routes into the US exist occasionally as cross-border trips posted from the Canadian side, but there is no US network — an American searching for a Los Angeles–San Francisco or LA–Vegas seat will find nothing.</p>

  <h2>The US equivalent</h2>
  <p>The same model exists in the United States, region by region rather than nationally. On the West Coast, <strong>Seat Sherpa</strong> covers California and Nevada — the same book-a-seat-in-a-car-already-going flow Poparide users know: drivers post trips they're making anyway, prices are capped at the real cost of the drive (the IRS mileage rate split across seats), and payment is held in-app until a 4-digit pickup code confirms the passenger is in the car.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>Poparide (Canada)</th><th>Seat Sherpa (US)</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Model</td><td>Cost-shared seats on trips drivers already take</td><td class="win">Identical</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Pricing</td><td>Suggested cost-sharing rates</td><td class="win">Hard-capped at IRS mileage rate ÷ seats</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Verification</td><td>Profiles + ratings</td><td class="win">Phone + government ID + driver's licence, two-way ratings</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Payment</td><td>In-app</td><td class="win">Paid in-app, held safely until pickup-code confirmation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Canada-wide</td><td>California &amp; Nevada</td></tr>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Crossing the border?</h2>
  <p>If your trip is genuinely cross-border — Vancouver to Seattle, say — neither platform solves it end to end today. The practical pattern travellers use: a Poparide seat to the last Canadian city, a bus or train across the border, and a carpool seat for the US leg where coverage exists. For trips entirely inside California or Nevada, a Seat Sherpa seat replaces the whole question.</p>

